index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..e9bcead83b7484e73611d31d188bd222dd551e7a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/arch/arm/pxa/h3970.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,141 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+H3970 DT resources and firmware handoff
+======================================
+
+The native ``hp-ipaq-h3970.dts`` describes hardware. The host RAM recipe applies
+a separate role overlay carrying the development boot arguments and static,
+slow SD fixture policy. This is not an arbitrary-reset initialization claim.
+The stock WinCE launcher supplies an initial peripheral state, and the RAM
+role retains ``clk_ignore_unused`` and ``fw_devlink=off``. Removing either flag
+needs evidence that all affected suppliers and consumers are owned correctly.
+
+Ownership inventory
+-------------------
+
+.. list-table::
+   :widths: 22 42 36
+   :header-rows: 1
+
+   * - Function
+     - Linux description and owner
+     - Remaining handoff or qualification gap
+   * - CPU and memory clocks
+     - CPU clock resources and OPP references constrain the PXA transition
+       driver; its fixed memory-clock rule and fast-bus control remain.
+     - CPU rail wiring/voltage ownership, settling/latency and H3970 bus-mode
+       behavior need independent evidence. No CPU supply is invented in DT.
+   * - Core interrupt/timer/PMU
+     - PXA interrupt controller, OS timer and CP14 PMU have SoC descriptions.
+       Old PMU descriptions retain legacy enumeration fallback.
+     - Physical timing, PMU and transition interactions need qualification.
+   * - Dock FFUART
+     - Default pinctrl owns RX34, CTS35, DSR37, TX39, DTR40 and RTS41. These
+       functions and directions match WinCE and Familiar startup images.
+       A boot-on, always-on fixed switch on ASIC3 B2 supplies the UART's
+       optional transceiver regulator. Both PXA UART drivers acquire it for
+       the registered port lifetime, including a closed console tty.
+     - Early console runs before ordinary consumer probing and still requires
+       the launcher to enable B2. GPIO36 and38 stay outside UART mux.
+   * - LCD
+     - PXA framebuffer resources, display timing and default pinctrl own
+       data58--73 and timing74--77. No panel power sequence is added.
+     - ASIC3 GPIO-B6/7/5/3/1 rails and enable remain inherited. WinCE boot
+       and Familiar runtime sequences differ; measure the exact path before
+       selecting a Linux power implementation. Disabled fixed switches record
+       the known rails without probing them. Backlight is also inherited.
+   * - Companion bus and providers
+     - Shared ASIC2/3 maps, GPIO providers, ASIC2 CCF clocks and external
+       oscillator descriptions coordinate component consumers.
+     - PXA CS5 mux and external-bus timing remain inherited. Do not copy a
+       complete old board initialization table over live firmware state.
+   * - ASIC2 IRQ and input
+     - GPIO/KPIO masks, pending bits, IRQ domain and EX0 clock are owned by
+       the explicit IRQ frontend. Touch and keys use input; ADC access is
+       shared with the IIO provider. Touch resolves its X/Y inputs through
+       named IIO consumer references in DT, validates the same companion,
+       and keeps complete panel-drive/conversion transactions atomic.
+     - Global mask/timebase registers 0x1600/04/08 retain firmware state;
+       mixed mask/reset-cause semantics need evidence before writes. Parent
+       edges, storms, pen timing and complete lifecycle need physical tests.
+   * - SD
+     - The ASIC3 child shares clock/control ownership. Native descriptions
+       use the TMIO request engine with either IRQ or per-request polled
+       completion. The original custom engine is isolated in an old-DTB
+       compatibility file. A request generation separates immediate reuse
+       of one request object from its preceding polling worker.
+     - GPIO4 edge and real transfer/error/reset behavior remain unqualified.
+       Slow, one-bit, read-only development limits and retained VDD stay.
+       Explicit external power sequencing is currently unsupported. The RAM
+       role overlay owns its static-card, one-bit, 384 kHz fixture policy;
+       the driver retains independent read-only development safety limits.
+   * - Audio
+     - PXA I2S/PDMA, UDA1380, ASIC2 clock, ASIC3 codec power/reset and speaker
+       GPIO, plus a simple ASoC card, describe normal playback resources.
+       A polled GPIO input switch reports headphone insertion separately.
+     - The legacy fixture remains for old DTBs. Playback, close/reopen and
+       audible tones have been exercised in RAM. Capture, jack insertion,
+       automatic jack routing and sleep remain unqualified. ASoC's both-edge
+       GPIO IRQ request is not supported by the current ASIC2 frontend.
+   * - AC presence
+     - Standard gpio-charger reports active-low PXA GPIO8. No charge-control
+       or current-limit GPIO is acquired.
+     - Battery scaling, gauge, timer and charger control remain unresolved.
+   * - Other peripherals
+     - The disabled USB controller describes PXA20 pull-up and ASIC2 GPIO10
+       VBUS sense. Disabled Bluetooth transport pinctrl and IR/Bluetooth
+       switches record known resources. GPIO line names retain established
+       assignments without requesting or driving lines.
+     - GPIO16 NOR VPP and charging controls must remain untouched. Stock NOR
+       stays the recovery anchor; this source work authorizes no flash write.
+   * - Lifecycle
+     - Component drivers own their acquired resources and documented
+       compatibility paths. Changes use one common platform configuration.
+     - Probe/defer/failure, warm kexec, removal and system sleep must be
+       qualified per device. A C860 result does not qualify an H3970.
+
+Pinmux evidence and scope
+-------------------------
+
+The H3900 Familiar ``h3900-init.h`` assigns the six UART pins above to AF1
+inputs or AF2 outputs and assigns all twenty LCD pins to AF2 outputs. The
+stock WinCE startup GAFR and GPDR images agree. In particular, GPIO36 is AF0
+output and GPIO38 is AF0 input; assigning the usual FFUART DCD/RI functions
+would change the board's inherited configuration.
+
+PXA pinctrl sets only the selected GAFR/GPDR fields and tracks their low-power
+direction through the existing MFP helper. These default groups do not write
+PGSR, output levels, rails or transceiver power. Sleep/wake behavior remains
+unqualified; no new sleep-state policy is described. Explicit default states
+make ordinary peripheral probing responsible for these pin functions while
+retaining the firmware power handoff.
+
+Acceptance boundary
+-------------------
+
+Schema, source comparison and framework/emulator checks establish software
+contracts. They do not establish rail order, electrical levels, FIFO/status
+semantics, arbitrary initial state or physical boot/recovery. Keep failures
+and their exact inputs, then qualify matched kernel/DT artifacts independently
+on each device before changing a production source pin or accepted artifact.
+
+Audio lifecycle ownership
+-------------------------
+
+The normal card uses the generic amplifier's existing 20 ms deferred supply
+disable. DAPM regulator handles belong to their widgets, rather than to the
+component device's devres stack: widgets are created during card binding,
+after the component itself registered. Device devres order must not release
+their handles before card shutdown, and card unbind must release them even
+when the component remains registered.
+
+Regulator consumer release waits for already requested deferred disables to
+finish before removing the handle. It does not shorten the quiescence delay,
+force a shared supply off, or make normal DAPM power-down synchronous. A failed
+disable still leaves an enabled reference that the consumer must resolve;
+release is not a substitute for handling that error.
+
+Framework tests exercise pending and active playback detach, card rebind,
+repeated disable cycles and shared-supply accounting with fake GPIO, I2C,
+clock and regulator providers. These establish software lifetime behavior,
+not physical DMA, audio, arbitrary supplier removal, kexec or sleep behavior.
