index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a9d31ca0fc8934a1f258e4d46d3b514d5bdd8ff8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/cpu-freq/pxa2xx.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+PXA2xx CPU frequency constraints
+===============================
+
+The PXA-specific CPUFreq driver owns transitions on PXA25x and PXA27x.
+Device Tree supplies CPU core and memory-timing clocks, an optional CPU
+regulator and available OPPs. Linux still uses the existing PXA clock code,
+transition notifiers, exact rate checks and failed-rate restoration. This
+is not a conversion to the generic cpufreq-dt transition implementation.
+The PXA2xx SoC compatibles are blocked from generic cpufreq-dt enumeration,
+so a configuration that includes both drivers cannot choose the generic
+transition path merely because the CPU now references an OPP table.
+
+Rates and memory timing
+-----------------------
+
+The supported exact PXA25x rates are 99,532,800, 199,065,600, 298,598,400 and
+398,131,200 Hz. PXA27x rates remain 104, 156, 208, 312, 416, 520 and 624 MHz.
+Available DT OPPs must be a subset of those rates. An unsupported available
+entry fails setup rather than rounding to an implemented rate. Disabled
+OPPs and absent rates are excluded from the policy. PXA27x retains its
+``pxa27x_maxfreq`` limit, with the existing 416 MHz default.
+
+The memory-timing reference must equal the value required by the existing
+transition. The driver filters the initial table and checks again before
+any transition. It does not implement memory retiming. On PXA27x this
+separates the 104/156 MHz core rates, which require a 104 MHz memory clock,
+from the higher core rates, which require 208 MHz. DT cannot bypass that
+restriction.
+
+PXA25x's two 398 MHz configurations still share one OPP. ``fast_bus`` keeps
+its existing default, boot parameter and runtime control. The PXA clock
+provider retains the run/turbo and memory-refresh programming sequences;
+changing the mode uses the existing transition notifiers and also checks
+the OPP and memory constraints. A PXA turbo clock configuration does not
+mean an OPP boost mode. Boost, interconnect and required power-domain OPP
+transitions are not implemented and their descriptions are rejected.
+
+Suppliers, voltage and compatibility
+-----------------------------------
+
+CPU ``clocks`` must contain ``core`` and ``memory``, in that order. A
+malformed explicit description fails rather than falling back to global
+clock aliases. Missing suppliers defer the platform driver's probe.
+``CLK_MEMORY`` exposes the existing memory-timing clock without changing
+its legacy global alias or the old clock IDs.
+
+A described ``cpu-supply`` is acquired from the CPU device. Its OPPs must
+include a valid voltage or voltage triplet. The driver applies the DT
+voltage range, intersecting it with the existing PXA27x voltage envelope.
+For PXA25x the board must provide electrically qualified voltage limits;
+this source conversion does not qualify a new regulator or voltage.
+Voltage is raised before increasing the frequency and reduced only after
+a successful decrease. A failed reduction retains the higher voltage and
+does not report a failed frequency change. Notifier or clock failures do
+not lower the voltage; a rate-readback mismatch attempts the existing
+clock rollback and reports failure.
+
+Without ``cpu-supply``, the existing board/firmware rail contract remains:
+PXA25x does not acquire or program a regulator, and PXA27x retains the
+legacy ``vcc_core`` lookup. A frequency-only OPP retains legacy PXA27x
+voltage bounds. OPP voltage properties alone do not establish a controllable
+rail. Old DTBs without CPU clocks or OPP references and legacy board boots
+retain global clock lookup and the implemented frequency tables.
+
+OPP availability is checked again before each target or same-frequency
+mode change. The policy table is constructed at probe; adding OPPs later
+requires reprobe. No new suspend OPP, thermal/energy model or dynamic table
+rebuild is implemented. The existing transition-latency assumption is
+retained rather than replacing it with unmeasured DT latency values.
+
+Evidence and qualification
+--------------------------
+
+KUnit exercises the real OF OPP parser, CCF, regulator and PXA notifier
+paths on fake providers. It checks legacy tables, missing/disabled/exact
+rates, memory partitions and caps, supplier deferral, malformed resource
+or voltage descriptions, transition ordering and failures, disabled
+runtime targets and same-frequency mode eligibility. These checks do not
+simulate PLL locking, voltage settling, SDRAM refresh, serial or DMA
+quiescence, firmware boot state or physical suspend/resume. Each device
+still requires independent RAM-boot and lifecycle qualification before
+production source pins are changed.
