diff --git a/Documentation/driver-api/gpio/asic2.rst b/Documentation/driver-api/gpio/asic2.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..5c4f3356aa20ddaac5dae07e3e52e440befba182
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/gpio/asic2.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+ASIC2 GPIO and interrupt ownership
+==================================
+
+ASIC2 uses one shared companion regmap. Its GPIO function may also register
+an IRQ domain for the GPIO and KPIO banks when firmware describes an
+interrupt controller, parent signal and EX0 clock. Descriptions without those
+resources keep their GPIO-only behavior and do not touch IRQ registers.
+The touchscreen can use a pen-detect interrupt while retaining polling for
+tracking and release. Encoded keys remain polled.
+
+The GPIO line namespace contains sixteen pins. The IRQ namespace is distinct:
+GPIO inputs 0--11 use IRQ numbers 0--11, KPIO key inputs 0--11 use 32--43, and
+special KPIO functions use 48--55. Use the definitions in
+``dt-bindings/interrupt-controller/compaq,asic2.h``. GPIO 12--15 and the gaps
+are not interrupt sources. Special KPIO sources use level-high flow; ordinary
+GPIO/KPIO inputs support a single rising/falling edge or a high/low level.
+Both-edge emulation and IRQ wake routing are not implemented.
+
+The IRQ domain is an immediate consumer of the GPIO frontend. Requests made
+before initialization completes return probe-defer. GPIO edge-event
+requests use ``gpiod_to_irq()``; IRQ resource acquisition locks the input
+against output changes, and both GPIO and KPIO users retain the frontend's
+module until their IRQ is released. Firmware IRQ consumers may also reference
+the GPIO function's interrupt-controller phandle directly with the IRQ
+namespace above.
+
+Controller takeover and handoff
+-------------------------------
+
+An explicitly described IRQ controller takes ownership of both bank-enable
+masks. Setup starts them masked and acknowledges stale valid pending bits.
+KPIO enable at 0x21c is never read: one lock-protected software shadow covers
+all KPIO consumers. Status reads and write-one-to-clear acknowledgements
+share bank offset 0x10; acknowledgements are direct mask writes. GPIO enable
+updates preserve bits outside the implemented twelve-source range.
+
+Type changes affect only a requested source's IRQ selectors. Setup and
+teardown do not change GPIO direction, output data, alternate functions,
+serial/display rails, reset-cause state or the global interrupt/timebase
+configuration. EX0 is acquired through CCF independently of EX1-derived ADC,
+SD and audio clocks; the provider restores inherited gate state on a matched
+disable. Its new clock ID is appended after the four existing IDs.
+
+The global interrupt/timebase configuration remains a firmware handoff
+dependency in this stage. The provider does not establish initialization from
+an arbitrary reset state. Keep this distinct from GPIO/KPIO mask ownership;
+removing that handoff dependency requires separate silicon and device evidence.
+
+Dispatch drains enabled sources from both banks without holding the shared
+mask lock across child handlers. After a fixed pass limit, the final active
+sources are masked until their consumers re-enable them; other users remain
+enabled.
+A failed mask/unmask write suppresses software dispatch until the consumer is
+re-enabled; this does not prove the physical line was masked after an I/O
+failure. Failed type/status operations report errors. Cleanup detaches the
+parent handler, waits for in-flight IRQ/RCU users, quiesces the bank masks
+and removes mappings while the GPIO
+chip and EX0 reference are still alive.
+
+Evidence and qualification
+--------------------------
+
+ARM KUnit tests use real IRQ domains, a simulated parent IRQ descriptor,
+hard-IRQ work, gpiolib and CCF over fake registers. They cover ownership,
+write-only shadow arbitration, per-source types and acknowledgements,
+shared-bank/reentrant dispatch, bounded storms, I/O/setup errors and teardown.
+The fake bus models selected software contracts, not electrical levels,
+interrupt synchronization or clock startup.
+
+The per-source acknowledge masks agree with both Familiar handlers and the
+stock H3970 WinCE handler. That source agreement is not physical IRQ proof.
+Qualify parent polarity, repeated edges, input behavior, cold/handoff clock
+state, probe failure, kexec and suspend/resume on each exact device before
+accepting the feature for production. No result on one PXA board qualifies
+another.
+
+Touchscreen consumer
+--------------------
+
+A touchscreen with ``pen-gpios`` owns the pen input exclusively through
+gpiolib. Its active polarity comes from the descriptor. Without that property,
+old DTBs retain the raw ASIC2 GPIO5 read and their existing sampling sequence.
+An optional pen interrupt must map to that same GPIO. Missing providers defer
+probe; a malformed explicit IRQ or a different GPIO/IRQ pair fails setup.
+The pair is validated before changing GPIO direction.
+Native H3970 describes GPIO5, active-low, with its falling-edge IRQ. This
+matches the legacy ASIC2 touchscreen source; physical parent/pen timing
+qualification is still independent.
+
+The IRQ is requested disabled and enabled only while the input is open.
+A threaded IRQ invokes the same serialized sampling function as the poller.
+Before driving the panel the driver masks the pen IRQ, then keeps it masked
+while the pen is held. The existing8ms poller tracks movement and release;
+a confirmed pen-up rearms the interrupt. A queued IRQ does not resample a
+press that polling has already consumed. Setting the poll interval to zero
+also suppresses IRQ sampling, so the existing polling-disable interface does
+not leave an interrupt-started press without release tracking.
+
+The ADC transaction still covers X/Y drive, four conversions per axis,
+settling, stop and pen restore under the shared lock. The GPIO descriptor
+does not replace or shorten that transaction. Read or ADC errors do not
+report a false press; polling remains available for recovery. The ADC clock
+keeps its existing bound-device lifetime.
+
+Close masks the interrupt and waits for its thread outside the ADC mutex.
+Remove and shutdown permanently stop sampling and drain IRQ work before
+input, IRQ, GPIO and clock resources disappear. Late polls/IRQs cannot
+restart a stopped device. This source ordering and fake-provider tests do
+not establish physical suspend, wakeup, hot-remove or kexec qualification;
+no IRQ wake support or arbitrary-reset initialization is added.
