diff --git a/Documentation/driver-api/mmc/asic3.rst b/Documentation/driver-api/mmc/asic3.rst
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3423b7e4af92437ef0003eeb4a300f2ada770240
--- /dev/null
+++ b/Documentation/driver-api/mmc/asic3.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,132 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
+
+ASIC3 SD host and TMIO integration
+==================================
+
+The ASIC3 development host in ``drivers/mmc/host/h3970-sd.c`` uses the
+TMIO request engine when firmware supplies a completion interrupt. Existing
+descriptions without an interrupt retain the polled backend. Both paths keep
+``compaq,asic3-mmc`` and the legacy ``compaq,h3970-asic3-sd`` compatible;
+there is no diagnostic-mode compatible or software-policy DT property.
+An explicitly malformed or deferred interrupt cannot silently select polling.
+
+The native H3970 description still selects polling. Its SD signal uses PXA
+GPIO4, separate from the ASIC2 parent interrupt; historical sources disagree
+on the edge in some board states. Physical IRQ and transfer evidence is
+required before adding that resource to the native board description.
+
+Hardware description and development policy
+------------------------------------------
+
+Standard MMC properties describe the board's wiring and capabilities. The
+adapter parses them, then imposes the existing development role: read-only,
+one-bit operation, one block, even 2--512 byte lengths, 192--384 kHz, no MMC
+or SDIO, and the existing scatterlist bounds. A lower supported firmware
+maximum is respected. The non-removable capability is diagnostic policy,
+not a claim that the socket is soldered down. Neither a successful build
+nor a four-bit wiring description enables wider or faster operation.
+
+ASIC3 owns external configuration, the shared parent map, CCF reference and
+slot power. TMIO owns the IRQ/PIO request engine and its timeout work. The
+adapter holds its SD clock and runtime-PM reference for the bound lifetime.
+It deliberately does not install generic TMIO runtime-PM callbacks. Slot VDD
+stays asserted across rescans and teardown, following the existing handoff
+contract. Explicit external supply or power-sequence properties currently
+fail before controller initialization: that ownership is not implemented.
+This limitation does not imply that such resources are invalid hardware.
+
+Register and response evidence
+-----------------------------
+
+With the TMIO control base at SD-window offset ``0x1000`` and
+``bus_shift = 1``, the command, argument, stop, block count, response, status,
+IRQ mask, clock, block length, card-option, error-detail, data and reset
+addresses coincide. Status2 is the high half of TMIO's merged status word,
+matching ASIC3's separate buffer-status register. Accesses are 16-bit at
+four-byte spacing. The other ASIC3 window-addressing variant is unsupported.
+
+The adapter supplies ASIC3 command encoding and high-half-first argument
+writes through an optional core callback. It preserves CMD55's prefix and
+uses the special no-response encoding only for CMD0. Its completion proxy
+preserves the polled R2 trailing byte (``0xff``), unused short-response words
+and the caller's response on failed/no-response commands. The proxy also
+runs for core timeout/setup failures; core tracing precedes this final fixup.
+Other TMIO adapters retain their original encoding and response behavior.
+
+The aligned, single-segment bounce buffer is necessary for the diagnostic
+role. Generic TMIO PIO assumes the current segment holds the block; a caller
+may instead split one block across odd-sized segments. The ASIC3 adapter
+restores the original scatterlist and copies only a complete successful
+read. A premature DATAEND cannot publish stale bounce data. No DMA path or
+multi-block/secondary command is enabled.
+
+Integration inventory
+---------------------
+
+.. list-table::
+   :widths: 22 78
+   :header-rows: 1
+
+   * - Area
+     - Implemented behavior and qualification gap
+   * - External configuration
+     - The original polled setup sequence is factored into common helpers.
+       The IRQ path prepares before reset, masks status sources and enables
+       its requested IRQ before host registration starts requests. Arbitrary
+       initial firmware states and real setup timing remain unqualified.
+   * - Command encoding
+     - CMD55, no-response commands and argument order are adapted. Register
+       writes are checked through the real core request path. Electrical
+       latch semantics and bus activity require hardware evidence.
+   * - Response packing
+     - The actual polled reader and adapter completions are compared for
+       NONE/R1/R1b/R2/R3/R6/R7. IRQ status and responses are injected in RAM.
+   * - Interrupt delivery
+     - Optional firmware IRQs select TMIO. No native GPIO4 edge is guessed.
+       Physical line polarity, acknowledgement, storms and missed IRQs
+       remain unqualified.
+   * - Reset and timeout
+     - ASIC3 uses 2 ms reset waits, its card-option word and a 300 ms request
+       timeout. Timeout recovery restores clock/options and retains VDD.
+       Its command CRC errors abort the data phase. Default TMIO adapters
+       retain 10 ms waits, their existing timeout and CRC-continuation rule.
+   * - Clock and VDD
+     - Both paths retain the slow diagnostic divider and bound CCF clock.
+       Generic set_ios supply switching is bypassed only for the adapter.
+       Physical clock, power/settling and external-supply ownership remain
+       unqualified.
+   * - Request limits
+     - Both paths share a pre-MMIO fence against data writes, known mutating
+       commands, write-mode CMD56 and opcodes outside the six-bit index.
+       The IRQ path also rejects secondary commands and invalid SG shapes.
+       This bounded contract does not qualify arbitrary vendor commands.
+   * - Capabilities
+     - Explicit firmware widths constrain generic TMIO. The ASIC3 diagnostic
+       ceiling is then reapplied after core capabilities and transfer limits.
+       No increase in supported physical protocol is claimed.
+   * - Card detection
+     - Existing diagnostic non-removable behavior is retained; card-change
+       IRQs stay masked. Physical removable-card behavior is unqualified.
+   * - Lifecycle
+     - Queued work completes before host removal; the core drains its work,
+       the parent IRQ is synchronized and runtime/CCF references are balanced.
+       Full platform probe/defer/failure, removal, kexec and system sleep need
+       independent device qualification before production use.
+
+Validation boundary and migration
+---------------------------------
+
+ARM KUnit exercises real MMC/TMIO request, IRQ, PIO and completion helpers on
+fake registers. It covers request fencing, response preservation, split SG
+reads, premature DATAEND, CRC policy, timeout reset, clock/power policy and
+stopping/busy completion. These are software checks. RAM does not emulate
+write-zero-to-clear status, FIFO advancement, the interrupt line or bus timing;
+the tests inject those events explicitly. Test counts are distinct from the
+ten-area inventory above and from physical function qualification.
+
+Keep parent resources shared and retain polling until the IRQ path has
+matched read/response evidence on the physical controller. Compare command,
+data/error recovery and teardown before changing the native board resources.
+Increasing width, frequency, write access or protocol support is a separate
+qualification decision. Production kernel pins and accepted artifacts do not
+change as part of this development integration.
